          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#History"} History
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesAlternativeTerminology"} Alternative terminology
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em Klein geometry
            is essentially that of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+space"} homogeneous space
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+space"}> coset space
            )
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
            in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Klein"} Felix Klein
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erlangen+program"} Erlangen program
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#History"} History
          %p
            Klein geometries form the
            %em local models
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometries"} Cartan geometries
          %p
            For the generalization of Klein geometry to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Klein+geometry"} higher Klein geometry
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong Klein geometry
            is a pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G, H)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is a closed Lie subgroup of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            such that the (left)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+space"} coset space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \coloneqq G/H
          %p
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+action"} transitively
            on the homogeneous space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\hookrightarrow G
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilizer+subgroup"} stabilizer subgroup
            of a point in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            See there at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "stabilizer+group#KleinGeometry"} Examples – Stabilizers of shapes / Klein geometry
          %h2#History History
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Klein1872"}> Klein 1872
            ) (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erlangen+program"}> Erlangen program
            ”) is first of all in section 1, considered the general idea of (what in modern language one would call) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            “of transformations” on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
          %blockquote
            %p by which the geometric properties of configurations in space remain entirely unchanged
          %p
            is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em Hauptgruppe
            = succeed "." do
              %em principal group
          %p
            Then in (
            %a{:href => "#Klein1872"}> Klein 1872, end of section 5
            ) it says:
          %blockquote
            %p Suppose in space some group or other, the principal group for instance, be given. Let us then select a single configuration, say a point, or a straight line, or even an ellipsoid, etc., and apply to it all the transformations of the principal group. We thus obtain an infinite manifoldness with a number of dimensions in general equal to the number of arbitrary parameters contained in the group, but reducing in special cases, namely, when the configuration originally selected has the property of being transformed into itself by an infinite number of the transformations of the group. Every manifoldness generated in this way may be called, with reference to the generating group, a body.
          %p
            This means in modern language, that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is the given group acting on a given space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \hookrightarrow X
            is a given subspace (a configuration), then the “body” generated by this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+space"} coset space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi Stab
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/Stab_G(S)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilizer+subgroup"} stabilizer subgroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Stab
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Stab_G(X)
            of that configuration. See also there at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "stabilizer%20group#KleinGeometry"} Stabilizer of shapes – Klein geometry
          %p
            The text goes on to argue that spaces of this form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi Stab
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/Stab_G(S)
            are of fundamental importance:
          %blockquote
            %p If now we desire to base our investigations upon the group, selecting at the same time certain definite configurations as space-elements, and if we wish to represent uniformly things which are of like characteristics, we must evidently choose our space-elements in such a way that their manifoldness either is itself a body or can be decomposed into bodies.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo =
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = E(n)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+group"} Euclidean group
              in
              = succeed "-dimensions;" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %mi O
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = O(n)
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} orthogonal group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Analogously, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mi Iso
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = Iso(d,1)
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
              of
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d+1)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = O(d,1)
              the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \mathbb{R}^{d+1}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
              itself.
            %p
              Passing to the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
              – by what physicists call
              %em gauging
              – yields the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
          %p
          #ExampleCrystallography.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallography"}> crystallography
                )
              %br/
              The study of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+groups"} crystallographic groups
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallography"} crystallography
              is much in the spirit of Klein geometry/the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erlangen+program"} Erlangen program
              (see for instance
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "Erlangen+program#Weyl38"} Weyl 1938
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "Erlangen+program#GruenbaumShephard10"} Grünbaum &amp; Shephard 2010
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "crystal#Engel86"} Engel 1986
            %p
              Concretely, the
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+orbifold"} quotient orbifold
              of the space of
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+vectors"} wave vectors
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momenta"} momenta
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystal
              lattice by the (dual)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+group"} crystallographic group
              is the
              = succeed "(-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+matter+theory"} condensed matter theory
              is formulated (see for instance the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron+energy+bands"} electron energy bands
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases"} K-theory classification of topological phases
